## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design multi-cloud Terraform architectures with per-tenant isolation, cross-cloud networking, and automated post-deployment validation including drift detection and reporting.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant Terraform Cloud workspaces with unique workspace keys
- Provider-agnostic networking module (AWS VPC, GCP VNet)
- Policy-as-code enforcement (OPA/Sentinel)
- Drift detection running after apply
- CI gates for tenant-scoped plans

## Code Example

```python
def detect_drift(desired_state, live_state):
    drift = {}
    for resource_id, desired_config in desired_state.items():
        live_config = live_state.get(resource_id)
        if not live_config:
            drift[resource_id] = "missing"
        elif live_config != desired_config:
            drift[resource_id] = {
                "expected": desired_config,
                "actual": live_config
            }
    return drift
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests how a junior engineer models read-only environments, data sourcing, and per-env state management without touching prod resources. It mirrors real-world patterns at scale at top tech companies.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform data sources for read-only references
- Per-env backends to segregate state
- Avoiding resource creation in isolated environments

## Code Example
```javascript
data "aws_vpc" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "tag:Environment"
    values = ["inventory"]
  }
}
data "aws_subnet" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "vpc-id"
    values = [data.aws_vpc.inventory.id]
  }
}
```

```javascript
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "inventory/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

